a while back I did a video specifically  about trolley memes and ten was a nice  number to start with so I did ten  trolley memes it was fairly well  received and I've been meaning to do  another video discussing various  iterations on on this particular thought  experiment the pandemic crisis that  we're now living in caused by Co vid 19  or the corona virus has indeed provoked  a lot of people unsurprisingly to create  new trolley memes that have to do with  that so I thought I would do a roundup  of those with a little bit of commentary  so here we go before we get started of  course this picture that you're seeing  right here is the traditional trolley  meme thought experiment in which you see  a trolley going down the track it will  kill five people unless you flip the  switch which will result in at killing  one and there's various ways of  understanding this the memes that follow  represent ways of taking this particular  thought experiment and making it do  something else so here they are  this first meme of course deals with the  question of flattening the curve you see  a flatter curve and a steeper curve with  more bodies more people being killed by  the coronavirus trolley and the question  is whether we're going to flip the  switch and by that we would engage in  all the measures that have been  recommended for flattening that curve  with the second meme we see another  choice but now it's a landlord and for  the next few memes we're gonna see  people who have power people who can  make choices that determine other  people's choices and outcomes being the  ones who are depicted in this so we have  a landlord a tenant who just came back  from China in nowadays it could be  really any place where coronavirus is  breaking out like New York and the  question is whether you're going to risk  infection now who are you gonna risk  infection for the family of the people  or hundreds of other people and it  depends on whether you allow them to  stay or kick them out both of them seem  to be problematic but from a utilitarian  perspective it seems better to allow  somebody to stay now doesn't it  now if you thought the previous one was  dark take a look at this one you are the  leader of a country which is highly  dependent on tourism dollars and it  reads in the trolley as a year's worth  of tourists from kovat nineteen stricken  countries heading for your lovely  capital the bottom track has the five  percent oldest population of your  country with weak immune system the  lever redirects tourist trolleys this  one and every single one after it back  to their country of origin the top track  however has a bag with a percentage of  your GDP equal to a year of your  country's tourism revenue will you pull  the lever so now it's between old  immune-compromised people's lives and  tourism dollars and now you know this is  one where you could say well that's an  easy decision to make of course we  should protect the old people but a  government is posed with some serious  issues particularly the more dependent  they are upon those tourism dollars as a  significant part of their GDP and  therefore part of their tax structure  Meem number four depicts a situation  that perhaps corresponds better to our  American situation in which some people  within the government have been saying  that it's very important that we protect  the economy above all and that some  older people some people at risk would  be willing to sacrifice themselves in  order for the economy to continue to  grow or at least not be as damaged as  much I particularly like this  formulation of the problem because it  kind of shows that if you do sacrifice  people for the economy what's the net  result going to be you're gonna ruin the  economy anyway as a result  here's another and perhaps darker  interpretation it reads you can stop the  trolley at any time but doing so would  disrupt the trolley service causing the  company to lose profits and so there's  no alternatives here other than whether  you flip the switch or not what this  represents is a view of the government  as really much more interested in  corporations than in protecting the  public which is a real failure and you  know it's a debatable issue I think that  you know the federal government of  America at this time and some of the  state governments could in fact be  rightly charged with having that  motivation and that prioritization of  large corporations over the welfare of  human beings  Meem number six is along the same lines  and I think this is perfectly framed for  those who are engaging in that line of  we've got to put the economy first which  really means not just putting the entire  economy but putting the sectors of the  economy that they favor first this is  from Bob Paxton he wrote aha now I get  it  America is doing the trolley problem but  some want to save the trolley and  there's two ways of thinking about this  one would be saying that the executive  here in America and also in our states  and in some of them and the legislators  are fundamentally mistaken in their  priorities about what they actually care  about this would be reading them as if  they were in some respect ignorant or  misguided we could also have a less  benign interpretation where we say you  know the trolley is it's a lousy health  care system in which insurance companies  have been allowed to dominate for a very  long time and for-profit medicine as in  effect displaced more publicly minded  medical centers and procedures those  would be the trolley that is going to be  running people over not just the virus  but a system that ought to be able to  but won't be able to protect people and  heal people from the virus  here's another meme I came across that  is smaller scale and a bit more oriented  at just being funny rather than making a  deep dark social policy point the lever  has a spittle from AK Ovid 19 patient do  you wash your hands before touching your  face what if it's like really annoying  it's kind of an absurdist thing because  you could easily you know whether you  wash your hands before touching your  face doesn't mean that you can't flip  the lever and save the people I think  it's simply pointing out how ridiculous  some of the things that we feel  discomforted or annoyed by are when it  comes to protecting ourselves and  protecting others  here's another one coming directly from  the Facebook page for trolley problem  memes saying we all need to do our part  to flatten the curve so please when  tying victims to the tracks  remember to space them at least six feet  apart so that our imaginary people and  the trolley problem aren't going to get  Co vid 19 and die of that rather than  dying of the trolley running them over  this is actually quite a good one as  well and I I don't see how anyone could  get offended at that unless they were  just simply choosing to take offence  because after all these are completely  imaginary people aren't they  now this meme and the one that follows  are not simply about the  coronavirus or covet 19 pandemic but  things that apply to trolley problems in  general and how they would fit in with  life and and I think this is a great one  there's us imagining the trolley problem  where we have agency but really the  trolley problem is more likely to be  about some other schmuck at the the helm  who and were stuck there on the tracks  in some place and we hope that he makes  the right decision  and here's the final meme in this series  another one having to do with the  conditions of trolley problems when we  see them in application and in this case  we actually do have some agency but it's  not a nice clean clearly delineated  problem as the thought experiment makes  it out to be  agency in real life ethical  decision-making is always messy and it  can often be screwed up by what other  people are doing or have decided to do  in the past and maybe didn't tell us  about all sorts of other 